{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgzC3i7BREF8ioKPNDtPV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It switches on pre-2.0 timestamp handling. In `kiln/timeparse.py:3`, `KILN_LEGACY_TZ=1` makes `parse()` read timestamps with no timezone as UTC. Without it, they're read as local time.\n\nFrom reading the code, the current tests don't depend on it. The only tests that call `parse()` are in `tests/test_timeparse.py`, and neither one is affected by the flag:\n- **Line 7** parses a timestamp that already has an offset (`+02:00`), so the flag never applies.\n- **Line 10** parses a timestamp with no timezone but only checks `.hour == 8`. The flag only attaches a timezone without shifting the time, so the hour is 8 either way.\n\nSo I think you're right that it's a leftover. Two caveats:\n- **I haven't run the tests** with and without the flag. Running commands needs approval, which this session can't give. A run of `python3 -m unittest` both ways would settle it.\n- **There's no recorded reason for it.** It arrived in the initial commit `d274cef` (\"Initial development setup\"), so git history doesn't say why it was added. Whoever wrote that setup may know of a case the tests don't cover, such as running on a machine whose local timezone isn't UTC.\n\nIf the tests pass without it, I can remove it from step 4."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":202,"cache_read_input_tokens":20062,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":202},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"<uuid-1>","uuid":"<uuid-36>","timestamp":"2026-11-13T03:08:42.671Z","request_id":"req_011CgzC3hxVfVmaEJRybNcpY"}
